course: physics
lesson: 5
title: Pushes and pulls all around us
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Every move you make is a push on something else that pushes you back: you walk forward by pushing the ground backwards.
explain: A swimmer pushes water back and the water pushes the swimmer forward; a horse pushes the road back with its hooves and the road pushes it on.
explain: Springy things store a push: a stretched rubber band or a trampoline springs back and gives the push back, while a lump of clay just goes splat.
explain: Forces do not only change speed: squeeze a sponge and you change its shape.
explain: Where you push matters too: a door opens easily when you push near the handle, far from the hinges, and hardly at all near them.
fact: A swimmer's hands and feet push water backwards, and the water pushes the swimmer forward; rowing and paddling work the same way.
fact: A duck's webbed feet push more water than plain toes would.
fact: When you walk, your shoe pushes the ground backwards and the ground pushes you forward, which needs grip; on wet ice your foot just slides back.
fact: Stretching a rubber band stores a pull, and it springs back when let go; never fire anything at people's faces, as even a paper pellet can hurt an eye.
fact: A trampoline's springs and mat stretch as you land and spring back, and most trampoline injuries happen when two people bounce at once.
fact: A bouncy ball squashes and springs back, while clay squashes and stays squashed, warming a little.
fact: No ball bounces back to the height it was dropped from, because some of its energy turns into heat and sound.
fact: Wind pushes on a sail, and the bigger the sail and the stronger the wind, the bigger the push; sailors can even sail partly against the wind by angling the sail.
fact: A horse pulls a cart because the ground pushes the horse forward harder than the cart holds it back, so on a slippery road it struggles.
fact: A force can change something's shape as well as its speed, as when you squeeze a sponge; let go, and the sponge springs back and sucks air back into its holes.
fact: A push far from a door's hinges turns it much more easily, so handles are put on the far side from the hinges.
fact: A heavy, fast bowling ball passes its push on to the pins, and the pins push each other; a light ball at the same speed would leave most pins standing.
exercise: What does a swimmer push against to move forward? | The water, pushing it backwards | The bottom of the pool | The air above the water | Nothing, swimmers float forward
exercise: What do you push on when you walk? | The ground, pushing it backwards | The air in front of you as you go | Your own legs, pushing from inside | Nothing, your feet just lift and land
exercise: Why can't you walk forward on wet ice? | Your foot slides back with no grip | Ice pushes you too far forward each step | Ice makes you heavier | Your legs get too cold to move
exercise: Why are a duck's feet webbed? | They push more water than plain toes | They keep the duck warmer | They make the duck lighter | They help it hear better under the water
exercise: Where was the push stored before the paper ball flew from the rubber band? | In the stretched band | In the paper ball | In the air around it | In your eyes as you aim
exercise: Why does a trampoline bounce you up? | Its springs and mat stretch, then spring back | The air under it pushes you up | It is lighter than you | It makes gravity weaker for a moment
exercise: When do most trampoline injuries happen? | When two people bounce at once | When you bounce only very low down | When it is a hot, sunny summer day | When you jump right in the middle
exercise: Why does a bouncy ball bounce but a lump of clay go splat? | The ball springs back; the clay stays squashed | The clay is pulled harder by gravity | The ball is lighter than air | The clay is too cold to bounce
exercise: Why doesn't a ball ever bounce as high as where you dropped it? | Some of its energy turns into heat and sound | The air pushes it down on the way up | Gravity is stronger near the floor | The floor keeps some of the ball's weight each time
exercise: How can a sailing boat move with no engine? | The wind pushes on its sail | The water pulls it along behind | The sail makes the boat lighter | The mast pushes back on the water
exercise: What do clever sailors do to sail partly against the wind? | Angle the sail | Take the sail down | Row backwards | Make the sail smaller and wetter
exercise: When a horse pulls a cart, what pushes the horse forward? | The ground, as its hooves push back | The cart pushing the horse from behind | The harness pulling the horse | The wind at its back
exercise: Can a push change a shape as well as a speed? | Yes, like when you squeeze a sponge | No, pushes only change speed | No, only heat can change a thing's shape | Only if the thing is moving
exercise: Why is a door handle never next to the hinges? | A push far from the hinges turns the door more easily | Hinges would get dirty from hands | It looks nicer on the far side | The door is lighter far from the hinges
exercise: Why does a heavy bowling ball knock down more pins? | It has a bigger push to pass on | It is rounder than a light ball | It rolls more slowly and carefully | Pins are pulled towards heavy balls
exercise: What would a light ball at the same speed do to the pins? | Bounce off and leave most pins standing | Knock all the pins down | Go straight through the pins without stopping | Stop dead before the pins
test: Little bro says you walk by pushing the air out of the way. right? | No, you push the ground back and it pushes you on | Yes, walking is pushing air | Yes, that is why wind stops us | No, your legs pull you along
test: What would happen if you tried to walk on perfectly slippery ice? | Your feet would slide and you would not move forward | You would walk faster than usual | You would float above it | You would walk backwards
test: it's just a paper pellet lol, flick it at his face. ok? | No, even paper can hurt an eye | Yes, paper is far too light to hurt | Yes, if it is a small pellet | No, but only because it is rude
test: You drop a ball from 1 metre. Which height could it bounce back to? | A bit less than 1 metre | Exactly 1 metre, the same height | More than 1 metre, a bit higher | Exactly 2 metres, twice as high
test: Why would bouncing on a trampoline with a friend be risky? | Your bounces clash and throw you badly | Two people together make the springs snap | It makes gravity stronger | The mat becomes too slippery
test: In a film a horse pulls a heavy cart up an icy road with ease. What is wrong? | On ice the horse's hooves would slip and it would struggle | Nothing, horses pull best on ice | The cart would be lighter on ice | Horses do not push on the ground
test: You try to open a door by pushing right next to the hinges. What happens? | It is very hard to turn | It swings open fastest | It comes off its hinges | It opens the wrong way
test: Why do rowers pull their oars back through the water? | To push the water back so it pushes the boat on | To pull the water towards the boat | To splash the other rowers | To slow the boat down
test: A sponge is squeezed and let go. What happens? | It springs back and sucks in air | It stays squashed flat for ever after | It gets heavier than it was before | It becomes harder and stiffer than before
test: Which is the springy one? | A tennis ball | A lump of clay | A ball of wet dough | A blob of mud
test: How is a bowling alley like a row of dominoes? | Each thing passes its push on to the next | Each thing pulls the one behind | Each thing gets heavier and heavier as it falls | Each thing stays perfectly still
exam: Identify the principal reason for which a pedestrian is enabled to progress forwards. | The ground exerts a forward force in response to the backward push of the foot | The air ahead is displaced by the motion of the body | The legs generate a force entirely within the body | The gravitational attraction acts in the forward direction
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of a force applied to a sponge? | It alters the shape of the sponge | It alters only the speed of the sponge | It has no effect upon a body at rest | It increases the mass of the sponge
exam: It is given that a ball is released from a certain height upon a hard floor. Ascertain the height of its rebound. | Somewhat less than the original height | Precisely the original height | Somewhat greater than the original height | Precisely double the original height
exam: Determine the position at which a door is most readily turned upon its hinges by a given push. | At the edge most remote from the hinges | Immediately adjacent to the hinges | At the exact middle point between the hinges | The position is of no consequence
